<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Bautista Sacasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Bautista Sacasa was a Nicaraguan Liberal physician and politician who led the Constitutionalist rebellion of 1926–1927 against the Conservatives. Elected president in 1932, he took office as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marines withdrew, but real power passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a>, commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a> and his nephew by marriage. Somoza forced him out of office in 1936 and took the presidency himself.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-sacasa.jpg" alt="Juan Bautista Sacasa" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Underwood &amp; Underwood · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Bautista_Sacasa_cph.3a44780.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sacasa was born in León on 21 December 1874, the eldest son of Roberto Sacasa and Ángela Sacasa Cuadra, his father's cousin twice removed. From 1889 to 1901 he studied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and earned a medical degree from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>. Back in León he taught at the National University, where he became dean of faculty, and he supported the Liberal regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-santos-zelaya">José Santos Zelaya</a>. He married María Argüello Manning, a cousin of the later president Leonardo Argüello.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1924 Sacasa joined a political coalition headed by the moderate Conservative Carlos Solórzano, and soon afterwards the United States Marines, who had been in Nicaragua for thirteen years, withdrew. In October 1925 the former president General Emiliano Chamorro overthrew Solórzano's government, and Sacasa fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Chamorro failed to win recognition from the United States and resigned in favour of Adolfo Díaz.</p><p>After an uprising of Liberal soldiers at Puerto Cabezas, Sacasa returned in 1926, claimed to be the constitutional president and set up a government there. Armed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, the Liberal forces under General José María Moncada came close to taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> forced both sides into talks. The resulting Pact of Espino Negro required both armies to disarm and let Díaz finish his term. Sacasa reluctantly gave up his claim but refused to sign and left the country, and Moncada signed for him. For the next six years Augusto Sandino led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war against the Marines who stayed to enforce the pact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Sacasa took office on 1 January 1933, the day before the Marines were due to leave. At the insistence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a> director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>. The next month he met Sandino, who pledged loyalty in return for an amnesty and land for his followers, but Sandino kept demanding that the Guard be disbanded and in February 1934 was assassinated on Somoza's orders.</p><p>Sacasa disapproved but could not check Somoza. His standing fell as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> prices collapsed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> and fraud was alleged in the congressional elections of 1934, while Somoza built ties with Moncada and Chamorro. Early in 1936 the Guard purged local officials loyal to the president, and on 9 June Somoza forced him to resign. Sacasa went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, where he died on 17 April 1946.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan Bautista Sacasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in León, in what is now <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.44,-86.88&amp;year=1920" aria-label="León on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2164 1700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juan Bautista Sacasa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2614 1970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2614 1970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=12.44,-86.88&amp;year=1920">What was here in 1920? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
